WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.560
Two plots hold the same four species, a hundred individuals each.

2
00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:10.780
Can one be more diverse than the other?

3
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.540
Count the species, and they tie.

4
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.800
Look at the dots, and they do not look alike.

5
00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:20.403
Richness is the number of species present.

6
00:00:21.783 --> 00:00:24.423
Both plots have four, so richness calls it a tie.

7
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:29.103
But richness counts every species once, however rare it is.

8
00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:31.883
It ignores abundance entirely.

9
00:00:33.083 --> 00:00:37.143
Diversity also asks how evenly the individuals are shared.

10
00:00:38.423 --> 00:00:41.163
In plot A, every species holds the same share.

11
00:00:42.463 --> 00:00:46.703
In plot B, two species hold most of the individuals, and two are rare.

12
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:51.177
Here is a way to put richness and evenness on one scale.

13
00:00:52.717 --> 00:00:56.037
Imagine a community in which every species is equally common.

14
00:00:57.317 --> 00:00:59.957
How many such species would score the same as plot B?

15
00:01:01.077 --> 00:01:03.537
That count is the effective number of species.

16
00:01:04.717 --> 00:01:08.097
For plot A, the answer is four, the same as its richness.

17
00:01:09.517 --> 00:01:14.257
For plot B, it is fewer than four, because two species hold most of the individuals.

18
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:17.767
The measure has a dial, called the order.

19
00:01:19.027 --> 00:01:22.407
At order zero, every species counts once, however rare.

20
00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:28.067
As the order rises, common species count for more, and rare ones for less.

21
00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:36.147
Turn the dial for plot B. Its row of equal species shrinks, step by step, as its two

22
00:01:36.147 --> 00:01:37.567
rare species count for less.

23
00:01:38.987 --> 00:01:41.707
Plot A does not move, because nothing in it is rare.

24
00:01:42.817 --> 00:01:45.297
So the order you choose changes the number.

25
00:01:46.597 --> 00:01:51.597
At order zero, the effective number is just the richness, so plot B ties plot A.

26
00:01:52.857 --> 00:01:57.897
Above order zero, it equals the richness only when every species is equally common.

27
00:01:58.957 --> 00:02:00.797
Always say which order you used.

28
00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:06.247
Now swap plot B's colours, so the rare species become the common ones.

29
00:02:07.547 --> 00:02:14.047
Nothing changes. These indices use only counts, never names, so none of them sees

30
00:02:14.047 --> 00:02:15.207
which species are present.

31
00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:22.710
One last test. A storm sweeps through plot B and kills the same share of every

32
00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.810
species. Most individuals die.

33
00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:28.870
What happens to its effective number?

34
00:02:36.117 --> 00:02:37.917
Nothing happens to it.

35
00:02:38.877 --> 00:02:41.877
The counts drop, but every species keeps the same share.

36
00:02:43.117 --> 00:02:46.037
So the effective number stays exactly where it was.

37
00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:51.037
So a disturbance can change diversity in only two ways.

38
00:02:52.197 --> 00:02:54.197
It can change which species are present.

39
00:02:55.377 --> 00:03:00.297
Or it can change how evenly the individuals are shared, as the survivors regrow.

40
00:03:01.577 --> 00:03:05.857
Whether that raises or lowers diversity is a question for experiments.
